# `multigrid::Options`

Tuning of the smoother, the coarsening and the execution path of the V-cycle.

The smoother is a Chebyshev polynomial in the diagonally scaled operator, aimed at the upper part of its spectrum, which damps the high-frequency error that coarse grids cannot represent; it needs no inner products, so each level is a few whole-field expressions. `smoothing_degree` is the polynomial degree per pre- and post-smoothing and `smoothing_ratio` sets how much of the spectrum it targets. `coarsest_cells`, `max_levels` and `dense_limit` shape the hierarchy and the coarsest solve, and the two `native_*` flags choose a fused host path. The defaults suit most problems.

## Fields

### `smoothing_degree`

`std::int64_t`, default `3`.

Chebyshev degree of each pre- and post-smoothing. Raise for fewer iterations at more work per cycle.

### `coarsest_cells`

`std::int64_t`, default `64`.

Coarsening stops at or below this many cells (per lane).

### `smoothing_ratio`

`double`, default `4.0`.

The smoother targets `[2/ratio, 2.2]` of the scaled spectrum.

### `max_levels`

`std::int64_t`, default `32`.

Upper bound on the number of levels.

### `dense_limit`

`std::int64_t`, default `1024`.

A coarsest level of at most this many cells is inverted by pseudo-inverse (handling singular problems); a larger one is smoothed.

### `native_smoother`

`bool`, default `true`.

On the CPU, for fields that record no derivative, smooth with the fused native loop (the identical polynomial).

### `native_cycle`

`bool`, default `true`.

On the CPU, for fields that record no derivative, run the whole V-cycle on host arrays.

## Guidance

Passed to the `Hierarchy` constructor.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The smoothing degree. A higher degree smooths more per cycle, so the preconditioned iteration needs fewer iterations (at more work per iteration).

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(0));
  const std::int64_t n = 64;
  const std::vector<mg::Axis> axes = {{n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}, {n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}};
  const Series rhs = pluss::series::ones({n, n}, pluss::ScalarType::Float64);

  for (const std::int64_t degree : {1, 2, 3, 6}) {
    mg::Options options;
    options.smoothing_degree = degree;
  mg::Hierarchy h({.axes = axes}, rhs, options);
  krylov::CgOptions cg_options;
  cg_options.check_interval = 1;
  cg_options.max_iterations = 1000;
  const auto apply = [&](const Series &u) { return h.apply(u); };
  const krylov::CgResult r = krylov::pcg(apply, [&](const Series &z) { return h.vcycle(z); }, rhs, {-2, -1}, cg_options);
    out.writeln("degree", degree, ": iterations =", r.iterations);
  }
}
```

### Example 2

The coarsest size. Coarsening stops when a level has at most this many cells; a smaller value gives more levels and a smaller coarsest problem.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(0));
  const std::int64_t n = 64;
  const std::vector<mg::Axis> axes = {{n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}, {n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}};
  const Series rhs = pluss::series::ones({n, n}, pluss::ScalarType::Float64);

  for (const std::int64_t coarsest : {4, 64, 1024}) {
    mg::Options options;
    options.coarsest_cells = coarsest;
    const mg::Hierarchy h({.axes = axes}, rhs, options);
    out.writeln("coarsest_cells", coarsest, ": levels =", h.levels(), ", coarsest =", h.coarsest_cells());
  }
}
```

### Example 3

The smoothing ratio. It sets the lower end of the target interval of the Chebyshev smoother (`2/ratio`); a larger ratio targets a wider band of high frequencies.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  const std::int64_t n = 64;
  const std::vector<mg::Axis> axes = {{n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}, {n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}};
  const Series rhs = pluss::series::ones({n, n}, pluss::ScalarType::Float64);

  for (const double ratio : {2.0, 4.0, 8.0}) {
    mg::Options options;
    options.smoothing_ratio = ratio;
  mg::Hierarchy h({.axes = axes}, rhs, options);
  krylov::CgOptions cg_options;
  cg_options.check_interval = 1;
  cg_options.max_iterations = 1000;
  const auto apply = [&](const Series &u) { return h.apply(u); };
  const krylov::CgResult r = krylov::pcg(apply, [&](const Series &z) { return h.vcycle(z); }, rhs, {-2, -1}, cg_options);
    out.writeln("ratio", ratio, ": iterations =", r.iterations);
  }
}
```

### Example 4

The level limit. A cap on the number of levels leaves a larger coarsest problem.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(0));
  const std::int64_t n = 64;
  const std::vector<mg::Axis> axes = {{n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}, {n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}};
  const Series rhs = pluss::series::ones({n, n}, pluss::ScalarType::Float64);

  for (const std::int64_t levels : {2, 4, 32}) {
    mg::Options options;
    options.max_levels = levels;
    const mg::Hierarchy h({.axes = axes}, rhs, options);
    out.writeln("max_levels", levels, ": levels =", h.levels(), ", coarsest =", h.coarsest_cells());
  }
}
```

### Example 5

The dense limit. A coarsest level with at most this many cells is inverted exactly; a larger one is only smoothed by a high-degree polynomial. With a coarsest level of 16 cells the two choices give the same iteration count here, because the polynomial solves so small a problem accurately; the exact inverse matters for large coarsest levels and for singular problems, where it is the pseudo-inverse that handles the null space.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(0));
  const std::int64_t n = 64;
  const std::vector<mg::Axis> axes = {{n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}, {n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}};
  const Series rhs = pluss::series::ones({n, n}, pluss::ScalarType::Float64);

  for (const std::int64_t dense : {4, 1024}) {
    mg::Options options;
    options.dense_limit = dense;
    options.coarsest_cells = 16;
  mg::Hierarchy h({.axes = axes}, rhs, options);
  krylov::CgOptions cg_options;
  cg_options.check_interval = 1;
  cg_options.max_iterations = 1000;
  const auto apply = [&](const Series &u) { return h.apply(u); };
  const krylov::CgResult r = krylov::pcg(apply, [&](const Series &z) { return h.vcycle(z); }, rhs, {-2, -1}, cg_options);
    out.writeln("dense_limit", dense, ": iterations =", r.iterations);
  }
}
```

### Example 6

The native smoother. The fused host loop and the expression path apply the identical polynomial: their results agree to rounding.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(12));
  const std::int64_t n = 64;
  const std::vector<mg::Axis> axes = {{n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}, {n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}};
  const Series rhs = pluss::series::ones({n, n}, pluss::ScalarType::Float64);

  mg::Options fused;
  mg::Options expression;
  expression.native_smoother = false;
  const mg::Hierarchy a({.axes = axes}, rhs, fused);
  const mg::Hierarchy b({.axes = axes}, rhs, expression);
  out.writeln("difference =", (a.vcycle(rhs) - b.vcycle(rhs)).abs().max().item<double>());
}
```

### Example 7

The native cycle. The whole V-cycle on host arrays against the same cycle as `Series` expressions: the same operator, so the same output.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(12));
  const std::int64_t n = 64;
  const std::vector<mg::Axis> axes = {{n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}, {n, 1.0 / n, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}};
  const Series rhs = pluss::series::ones({n, n}, pluss::ScalarType::Float64);

  mg::Options host;
  mg::Options expressions;
  expressions.native_cycle = false;
  expressions.native_smoother = false;
  const mg::Hierarchy a({.axes = axes}, rhs, host);
  const mg::Hierarchy b({.axes = axes}, rhs, expressions);
  out.writeln("difference =", (a.vcycle(rhs) - b.vcycle(rhs)).abs().max().item<double>());
}
```

